{
  "abstract": "Let f(y) be the decimal reversal of the y-th prime (f(1) = 2, f(2) = 3, f(5) = 11, f(11) = 13). OEIS A100475 is the orbit of 1 under f, and its entry asks (Jonathan Vos Post, 2004) whether the orbit of any other positive start ever enters a loop. This project proves that it never does, i.e. the negation of the formal-conjectures statement OeisA100475.conjecture: the number of digits never decreases along an orbit (p_y > y, and a prime never ends in 0), so on a cycle it is a constant D and every cycle element v satisfies p_v < 10^D; for D <= 4 this forces v <= 1229, and a kernel-checked table of the first 1230 primes with an escape-time ranking shows that no cycle stays below 1230; for D >= 5 it contradicts the bound pi'(10^D) < 10^(D-1) on the number of primes below 10^D, proved for D = 5, ..., 8 by Legendre's sieve recursion evaluated by the Lean kernel and for D >= 9 by Chebyshev's bound theta(x) <= x log 4 with the split point 10^(D-2).",
  "authors": [
